{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>If these die like every man dies</b> — Behold, many among the people also said to him many times, \"Why have you brought us up from Egypt?\" But the punishment upon these men was beyond that of any man, for they said, \"Is it a small thing that you have brought us up out of a land flowing with milk and honey to kill us in the wilderness, that you must also make yourself a prince over us?\" (Numbers 16:13), for they committed two evils: they belittled the honor of the master, and they denied every deed of YHWH that He did in Egypt and in the wilderness, and also the standing at Mount Sinai, of which it was said, \"and in you they shall believe forever\" (Exodus 19:9) — yet they said that he was not worthy to rule over them and that only evil had come to them through him. And because of this it says, \"And Moses was very angry\" (Numbers 16:15). And Korah too said, \"Why do you lift yourselves up?\" (Numbers 16:3), and therefore his tent was swallowed up with them. And this is the meaning of \"not YHWH sent me\" — that He did not send me at all to bring them out of Egypt, in the sense of \"whom YHWH sent to do in the land of Egypt\" (Deuteronomy 34:11), \"and this shall be the sign for you that I have sent you\" (Exodus 3:12). And likewise, \"For YHWH has sent me to do all these deeds\" (Numbers 16:28) — the deeds that you have seen with your own eyes; he alluded to everything he did from the day He said to him, \"Come now and I will send you to Pharaoh\" (Exodus 3:10), for the beginning of the prophet's coming is called a sending (shelichut), as in \"Whom shall I send and who will go for us?\" (Isaiah 6:8), \"And I sent before you Moses, Aaron, and Miriam\" (Micah 6:4). And the meaning of \"all the deeds\" is not the exchange of the firstborn for the Levites and the priesthood of Aaron, as the commentators say, but to encompass all the deeds that Moses did before the eyes of all Israel, as I have explained."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The phrase 'beyond that of any man' renders לְהָאָדָם; the sense is that their punishment exceeded the normal human fate, corresponding to the verse's idiom 'if they die like every man.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:22.675730950Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}